.TH NYMEA-MODBUS-CLI 1 "July 2026" "nymea-modbus-cli 1.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nymea-modbus-cli \- inspect, read, and write Modbus TCP, TLS, and RTU devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nymea-modbus-cli
.BI --address " address"
.RI [ TCP-options ]
.RI [ request-options ]
.br
.B nymea-modbus-cli
.BI --serial " device"
.RI [ RTU-options ]
.RI [ request-options ]
.br
.B nymea-modbus-cli
.BI --address " address"
.B --tls-info
.RI [ TLS-options ]
.br
.B nymea-modbus-cli
.B --list-serials
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nymea-modbus-cli
is a diagnostic command-line client for Modbus TCP, Modbus TCP over TLS, and
Modbus RTU. It can read input or holding registers and discrete inputs or
coils, write a value, inspect a TLS endpoint without sending Modbus traffic,
and list local serial ports.
.PP
Select exactly one transport using
.B --address
for TCP or
.B --serial
for RTU. A normal read or write also requires a register address. The
.B --tls-info
and
.B --list-serials
modes do not require one.
.SH GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " --help"
Display command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B --help-all
Display command-line help including generic Qt options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v , " --version"
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d , " --debug"
Print additional connection and protocol information.
.TP
.B --list-serials
List serial ports detected on the host and exit.
.SH TCP OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a , " --address " \fIaddress\fR
Connect to the specified Modbus TCP server IP address.
.TP
.BR \-p , " --port " \fIport\fR
Set the TCP port. The default is 502 for plain Modbus TCP and 802 when TLS is
enabled.
.TP
.B --tls
Use Modbus TCP over TLS.
.SH TLS OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --tls-version " version"
Select the TLS protocol. Supported values are
.B auto
and
.BR 1.2 .
The default is
.BR auto .
.TP
.BI --tls-fingerprint " sha256"
Accept only a server certificate whose DER-encoded SubjectPublicKeyInfo has
the specified SHA-256 fingerprint. Hexadecimal fingerprints may contain
colons or whitespace.
.TP
.B --tls-accept-any-fingerprint
Accept the SPKI fingerprint presented by the server during each handshake.
This disables server identity verification and cannot be combined with
.BR --tls-fingerprint .
The connection remains encrypted but is vulnerable to a man-in-the-middle
attack. Use this option only for controlled diagnostics.
.TP
.BI --tls-server-name " name"
Set the TLS server name used for Server Name Indication (SNI) and certificate
hostname verification.
.TP
.BI --tls-client-certificate " file"
Present the PEM client certificate in
.IR file .
The file may contain the leaf certificate followed by intermediate
certificates. This option requires
.BR --tls-client-key .
.TP
.BI --tls-client-key " file"
Read the PEM EC or RSA client private key from
.IR file .
This option requires
.BR --tls-client-certificate .
.TP
.BI --tls-client-key-passphrase-file " file"
Read the client private-key passphrase from
.IR file .
One trailing newline is ignored. This option requires
.BR --tls-client-key .
.TP
.B --tls-info
Perform a TLS handshake, print connection timing, negotiated protocol and
cipher, session information, and the server certificate chain, then exit
without sending Modbus traffic. This option enables TLS and uses port 802
unless
.B --port
is supplied.
.SH RTU OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --serial " device"
Use Modbus RTU through the serial
.IR device ,
for example
.BR /dev/ttyUSB0 .
.TP
.BI --baudrate " baud"
Set the serial baud rate. The default is 19200. Common values include 1200,
2400, 4800, 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600, and 115200.
.TP
.BI --parity " parity"
Set serial parity to
.BR none ,
.BR even ,
.BR odd ,
.BR space ,
or
.BR mark .
The default is
.BR none .
.TP
.BI --databits " bits"
Set the number of data bits to 5, 6, 7, or 8. The default is 8.
.TP
.BI --stopbits " bits"
Set the number of stop bits to 1, 1.5, or 2. The default is 1.
.SH MODBUS REQUEST OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m , " --modbus-address " \fIid\fR
Set the Modbus server address (unit or slave ID). The default is 1. Address 0
must be selected using
.BR --broadcast .
.TP
.B --broadcast
Send the request to Modbus server address 0. This option cannot be combined
with an explicit
.BR --modbus-address .
.TP
.BR \-t , " --type " \fItype\fR
Select the register type:
.BR input ,
.BR holding ,
.BR discrete ,
or
.BR coils .
The default is
.BR holding .
.TP
.BR \-r , " --register " \fIregister\fR
Set the first Modbus register address.
.TP
.BR \-l , " --length " \fIcount\fR
Set the number of registers or bits to read. The default is 1.
.TP
.BR \-w , " --write " \fIdata\fR
Write the decimal value
.I data
instead of performing a read.
.SH TLS AUTHENTICATION
Without
.BR --tls-fingerprint ,
normal CA and hostname validation is used. When validation fails, the CLI
prints the server SPKI-SHA256 fingerprint so it can be verified out of band
and supplied to a subsequent invocation.
.PP
The CLI does not save fingerprints, certificates, private keys, or TLS
sessions. A fingerprint passed with
.B --tls-fingerprint
is valid only for that invocation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read two holding registers over plain Modbus TCP:
.PP
.EX
nymea-modbus-cli -a 192.168.0.10 -p 502 -r 1000 -l 2
.EE
.PP
Read register 1000 over TLS 1.2 with an SPKI pin:
.PP
.EX
nymea-modbus-cli -a 192.168.0.10 --tls --tls-version 1.2 \\
    --tls-fingerprint 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef \\
    -r 1000
.EE
.PP
Inspect a TLS endpoint without sending Modbus traffic:
.PP
.EX
nymea-modbus-cli -a 192.168.0.10 --tls-info --tls-version 1.2 \\
    --tls-accept-any-fingerprint
.EE
.PP
Read two holding registers over Modbus RTU:
.PP
.EX
nymea-modbus-cli --serial /dev/ttyUSB0 --baudrate 9600 -r 1000 -l 2
.EE
.PP
Write decimal value 16 to holding register 200:
.PP
.EX
nymea-modbus-cli -a 192.168.0.10 -r 200 -w 16
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero after a successful request, TLS inspection, or serial-port list.
Returns non-zero for invalid arguments, connection or TLS failures, Modbus
errors, and unsuccessful reads or writes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/nymea-modbus-cli
Bash command completion definitions.
.SH SECURITY
Prefer a verified CA chain or an independently verified SPKI fingerprint.
Private-key passphrases should be supplied using
.B --tls-client-key-passphrase-file
rather than placed directly on the command line. Protect all client private
keys and passphrase files from other users.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR bash (1)
